{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Supersedes my earlier mutation-evidence comment: that evidence was incomplete, and the gap it hid is the interesting part.\n\nThe plan-review gate found that this ticket's own proving command is **vacuous**:\n\n> `grep -n \"not yet a declared support target\" .github/workflows/test.yml` returns nothing REGARDLESS of whether the fix is applied, because the falsified phrase is split across two physical comment lines.\n\nVerified directly:\n\n```\n$ git show origin/main:.github/workflows/test.yml | sed -n '331,332p'\n  # file-lock regression surfaces off the hot path. Non-blocking: Windows is not yet a\n  # declared support target, so a genuine incompatibility is a visible finding, never a\n\n$ git show origin/main:.github/workflows/test.yml | grep -c \"not yet a declared support target\"\n0\n```\n\nZero on the **unfixed** tree. The proof could never have failed, so it proved nothing.\n\n**My regression test had inherited the same flaw.** It substring-matched the raw file text, so it would have caught a reintroduction only if the sentence landed on a single line. Mutation 1 in my earlier comment passed for that accidental reason — I mutated one line, which put the whole phrase on one line, so the test looked stronger than it was. A revert that restored the original wrapping — the most likely real-world regression, since that is literally what `git revert` produces — would have sailed through green.\n\n**Fix:** the test now strips each comment line's `#`, joins them into one string and collapses whitespace before matching, so wrapping cannot hide the claim. The docstring records why, so the next person does not \"simplify\" it back into a substring search.\n\n**Mutation 4, the one that matters,** restores `origin/main`'s wrapped block verbatim:\n\n```\nrestored wrapped original; single-line grep finds: 0   (would evade a naive grep)\nFAILED test_windows_support_target.py::test_test_yml_does_not_assert_windows_is_unsupported[not yet a declared support target]\nFAILED test_windows_support_target.py::test_sweep_windows_comment_cites_the_adr_and_the_epic\n2 failed, 5 passed\n```\n\nTwo failures are correct here: the original block both asserts the falsified claim and cites none of the ADR/epic/story. Before the hardening this mutation produced **0 failures**.\n\nMutations 1-3 from the earlier comment still hold on the hardened test (single-line reintroduction, dropped `continue-on-error: true`, dropped ADR citation — one failure each, six passing), and the tree restores to `7 passed`.\n\nThe ticket's Testing section and AC1 have been rewritten to state the joined-and-normalised proof instead of the single-line grep. Worth noting for the rest of the epic: `grep` on a wrapped prose sentence in a comment is not a proof, and several sibling tickets carry proving commands of the same shape."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788576341291036216,"uuid":"c1363fdc-77fd-4a72-b2ea-4871a5094a38"}